---
title: Slow-baked salmon
description: Lesson 2.3. A gentle oven, and cooking to a temperature.
servings: 2
time: 45 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, the-oven]
lesson: "2.3"
technique: gentle roasting
status: draft
---

> Technique: gentle roasting. Fish sets at much lower temperatures than
> meat and goes from silky to dry within a few degrees. A low oven
> heats it slowly, so you can stop at the degree you want.

Dissolve @fine salt{45%g} in @cold water{1%litre} in a #dish{}. Put in @salmon fillets{2}(about 150 g each, skin on) and leave for ~{15%minutes}. Lift them out and pat them dry.

> Why brine: fifteen minutes in salt water seasons the flesh, helps it
> stay moist, and reduces the white stuff that otherwise appears on the
> surface.
-- source: Andrew Janjigian, "Two Tricks to Rid Salmon of That White Stuff", America's Test Kitchen, 2021-03-30, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3158-two-tricks-to-rid-salmon-of-that-white-stuff

Heat the oven to 140 °C (120 °C fan). Rub a #baking tray{} with @oil{1%tsp} and lay the fillets on it, skin side down.

Bake for about ~{18%minutes}, until the centre of the thickest part reads 52 °C on a #thermometer{}. Start checking after 12 minutes.

> Safety: 52 °C is a preference, not the official figure. The USDA's
> safe minimum for fish is 63 °C, read before it leaves the heat. To
> follow it, keep baking to 63 °C; the fish will be firmer and drier.
> America's Test Kitchen, whose 52 °C this is, calls the choice a
> personal decision about risk.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14 (read from an archive copy), https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart : fish and shellfish 145 °F (62.8 °C) ; "What's the Ideal Temperature of Cooked Salmon?", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how-tos/9999-ideal-temperature-cooked-salmon (farmed salmon 125 °F, wild 120 °F)

> Science: the white stuff is albumin, a protein dissolved in the
> fish's juices. Heat squeezes the juices to the surface, where the
> albumin dries and sets white; a low oven pushes out less of it. The
> hotter the fish gets, the more it loses: in one study salmon cooked
> in a 57 °C water bath kept 93.7% of its weight, while fillets
> oven-roasted to 70 °C in the centre lost 16.2%. Look up: albumin, protein denaturation, cooking
> loss.
-- source: "How To Roast Salmon Gently for the Silkiest Texture", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-10-06, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7551-how-to-roast-salmon-gently-for-the-silkiest-texture ; Głuchowski, Czarniecka-Skubina, Wasiak-Zys and Nowak, "Effect of Various Cooking Methods on Technological and Sensory Quality of Atlantic Salmon (Salmo salar)", Foods 8(8):323, 2019, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods8080323

> Why farmed and wild differ: wild salmon has about half the fat and
> more cross-linked collagen, so it is firmer and dries sooner. Take
> wild salmon out about 3 °C earlier.
-- source: "What's the Ideal Temperature of Cooked Salmon?", America's Test Kitchen (as above)

Serve with a squeeze of @lemon{1/2}.

> Next time, without the recipe: any thick fish fillet, a short brine,
> a low oven, and a thermometer. Decide the temperature first and stop
> when you reach it.
